# Dimensionality Reduction using PCA and LDA on Heart Disease Dataset

## Overview
High-dimensional datasets, especially in medical research, often contain redundant or correlated features, increasing computational complexity and making it harder to visualize or interpret the data. Dimensionality reduction methods such as **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)** and **Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)** are used to project such datasets into lower-dimensional spaces while preserving important information.

This project demonstrates the application of PCA and LDA on the **Heart Disease dataset**, reducing the data to two dimensions (PCA) and one discriminant component (LDA) for visualization and analysis.

---

## Dataset
- **Name:** Heart Disease Dataset  
- **Source:** [Kaggle – Heart Disease UCI](https://www.kaggle.com/ronitf/heart-disease-uci)  
- **Number of samples:** ~303  
- **Number of features:** 13 numeric features  
- **Classes:** 2 (Presence or absence of heart disease)  
- **Target:** Last column (label)

> The dataset was downloaded from Kaggle. All features are numeric and scaled before applying PCA and LDA.

---

## Data Preprocessing
1. Separate features (`X`) and labels (`y`)  
2. Standardize features using **StandardScaler** to ensure all features contribute equally  
3. Prepare the data matrix for linear projections  

> Feature scaling is essential for PCA because it is sensitive to the scale of the features.

---

## Methods

### Principal Component Analysis (PCA)
- **Type:** Unsupervised  
- **Goal:** Maximize variance  
- **Components:** 2  
- **Result:** 2D visualization of the dataset preserving overall variance

### Linear Discriminant Analysis (LDA)
- **Type:** Supervised  
- **Goal:** Maximize class separability  
- **Components:** 1 (binary classification: `C-1`)  
- **Result:** 1D projection highlighting class differences

> Since the dataset has only two classes, LDA extracts one discriminant component, which is visualized as a single axis.

